00:35:33.340 --> 00:35:36.300
But tell me more about the systems and levers

00:35:36.300 --> 00:35:39.300
and what else is in there

00:35:39.300 --> 00:35:41.180
because there's more than just that.

00:35:41.180 --> 00:35:43.300
Well, I mean, yes, there's more than that.

00:35:44.540 --> 00:35:46.220
So my co-author is Joel Tosi

00:35:46.220 --> 00:35:48.660
and he, the book started

00:35:48.660 --> 00:35:50.460
because he was already teaching a course

00:35:50.460 --> 00:35:52.780
called Something Systems Thinking Something

00:35:52.780 --> 00:35:55.300
and he was highlighting three key tools,

00:35:55.300 --> 00:35:58.340
which are key pillars within the book.

00:35:58.420 --> 00:36:01.340
Causal modeling, obviously, like you can't do,

00:36:01.340 --> 00:36:03.100
I don't know how to do systems thinking

00:36:03.100 --> 00:36:05.380
without thinking about the effects

00:36:05.380 --> 00:36:08.380
of aspects of the system or attributes

00:36:08.380 --> 00:36:09.900
on the system on one another.

00:36:09.900 --> 00:36:12.620
So causal modeling is the thing that allows you

00:36:12.620 --> 00:36:15.460
to sort of reason through a situation

00:36:15.460 --> 00:36:17.540
like better testing, worse quality,

00:36:17.540 --> 00:36:21.260
where, hey, if we increase the emphasis

00:36:21.260 --> 00:36:22.620
on testing over here,

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:25.260
what is the impact on that behavior over there?

00:36:25.260 --> 00:36:27.980
Causal modeling gives you almost a visual language

00:36:27.980 --> 00:36:29.740
to explore those ideas.

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:31.300
So that's a big part of it.

00:36:33.420 --> 00:36:36.820
The other thing, two things that Joel brought

00:36:36.820 --> 00:36:40.100
that I would never have thought about, honestly,

00:36:40.100 --> 00:36:45.100
I discarded the statistical process control stuff

00:36:45.100 --> 00:36:47.340
that I had encountered in the 90s

00:36:47.340 --> 00:36:50.860
and he was bringing a different lens to it

00:36:50.860 --> 00:36:52.660
than I had ever seen before

00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:55.460
with the way that he was using Schubert charts

00:36:55.500 --> 00:36:58.900
to reason about natural variability

00:36:58.900 --> 00:37:03.900
and one of his key points was to look at variability

00:37:04.340 --> 00:37:07.020
to make sure that you're not overreacting

00:37:07.020 --> 00:37:09.780
to what turns out to be a very natural variability

00:37:09.780 --> 00:37:12.700
within your organization,

00:37:12.700 --> 00:37:15.220
whether it's variability on things like bug counts

00:37:15.220 --> 00:37:17.860
or it's variability on things like cycle time,

00:37:18.860 --> 00:37:21.260
that one of the things we see happen a lot

00:37:21.260 --> 00:37:22.820
is people start pulling levers

00:37:22.820 --> 00:37:24.740
when it's actually super premature

00:37:24.740 --> 00:37:27.300
and in the process destabilize a system

00:37:27.300 --> 00:37:28.780
that is actually stable

00:37:28.780 --> 00:37:30.980
and instead they need to be looking at it

00:37:30.980 --> 00:37:33.420
a little bit more holistically

00:37:33.420 --> 00:37:35.620
as opposed to special cause,

00:37:35.620 --> 00:37:39.140
which is where there were reasons

00:37:39.140 --> 00:37:41.940
that we could actually go prevent in the future

00:37:41.940 --> 00:37:42.780
from happening.

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:46.580
So his lens on statistical process control

00:37:46.580 --> 00:37:48.140
I found really interesting

00:37:48.140 --> 00:37:52.460
and it dovetailed so nicely with causal diagrams

00:37:52.460 --> 00:37:53.740
because you can now reason

00:37:53.740 --> 00:37:56.100
about what are all of the contributing factors

00:37:56.100 --> 00:37:57.060
to high variability.

00:37:57.060 --> 00:37:59.340
High variability just becomes an attribute

00:37:59.340 --> 00:38:01.820
within that causal diagram.

00:38:01.820 --> 00:38:03.100
And then the third thing

00:38:03.100 --> 00:38:04.580
that he was bringing to the table

00:38:04.580 --> 00:38:06.380
that never would have occurred to me

00:38:06.380 --> 00:38:08.820
is more of an economics tool,

00:38:08.820 --> 00:38:10.300
it's U-curves,

00:38:10.300 --> 00:38:12.980
looking for the sweet spot

00:38:12.980 --> 00:38:15.380
by mapping the,

00:38:15.380 --> 00:38:16.980
we see this as an example of this

00:38:16.980 --> 00:38:17.980
would be a batch size,

00:38:17.980 --> 00:38:20.460
what is the appropriate batch size in the manufacturing

00:38:20.460 --> 00:38:21.940
and you've got one curve

00:38:21.940 --> 00:38:24.060
is showing you the holding cost

00:38:24.060 --> 00:38:28.100
and another curve is showing you the tear down cost

00:38:28.100 --> 00:38:31.220
and so the answer isn't zero or one,

00:38:31.220 --> 00:38:32.940
like the smallest batch size

00:38:32.940 --> 00:38:34.620
you could do of a thing is one

00:38:34.620 --> 00:38:36.060
but that's the wrong answer

00:38:36.060 --> 00:38:37.740
because it turns out that manufacturing

00:38:37.740 --> 00:38:40.460
the tear down cost is so high

00:38:40.460 --> 00:38:43.260
that that would not be the maximum efficiency.

00:38:43.260 --> 00:38:46.340
But if you go make seven years worth of a part

00:38:46.340 --> 00:38:47.860
in manufacturing,

00:38:47.900 --> 00:38:51.100
they will age and fall apart on the shelf

00:38:51.100 --> 00:38:53.900
and so you actually will not get the value from it

00:38:53.900 --> 00:38:56.420
so you gotta find for your context

00:38:56.420 --> 00:38:58.380
what the sweet spot is.

00:38:58.380 --> 00:39:00.060
Well, it turns out that that applies

00:39:00.060 --> 00:39:01.300
just as well to software

00:39:01.300 --> 00:39:04.900
just with different effects affecting costs.

00:39:04.900 --> 00:39:06.220
So whether it's,

00:39:06.220 --> 00:39:08.060
if you think of how much do we ship

00:39:08.060 --> 00:39:09.020
in a given release,

00:39:09.020 --> 00:39:10.140
what's our release cadence,

00:39:10.140 --> 00:39:11.260
what's our release frequency,

00:39:11.260 --> 00:39:12.940
how much are we shipping at a time,

00:39:12.940 --> 00:39:15.500
that's an example of a batch size problem

00:39:15.500 --> 00:39:19.260
that you curves are the perfect thinking tool

00:39:19.260 --> 00:39:20.580
to think through,

00:39:20.580 --> 00:39:23.980
what are our costs if we batch up a whole lot?

00:39:23.980 --> 00:39:25.740
Well, risk is in there

00:39:25.740 --> 00:39:28.380
and the various forms of risk,

00:39:28.380 --> 00:39:29.860
not just building the potential

00:39:29.860 --> 00:39:31.900
of building bad stuff on top of bad stuff

00:39:31.900 --> 00:39:32.740
and not knowing

00:39:32.740 --> 00:39:34.420
because we don't have the feedback loop

00:39:34.420 --> 00:39:35.660
but also the possibility

00:39:35.660 --> 00:39:37.260
that we're just building in the wrong direction

00:39:37.260 --> 00:39:39.860
and customers are not gonna want what we've got

00:39:39.860 --> 00:39:42.180
and the risk that by the time we actually release,

00:39:42.180 --> 00:39:44.780
it's out of date

00:39:44.780 --> 00:39:46.620
and we see,

00:39:46.620 --> 00:39:49.100
I think we see this in various software projects

00:39:49.100 --> 00:39:50.300
over the years,

00:39:50.300 --> 00:39:52.740
