00:45:55.940 --> 00:45:59.020
That it's the environment that created the potential

00:45:59.020 --> 00:46:01.500
for that one person forgetting

00:46:01.500 --> 00:46:05.300
to turn into a disaster of epic proportion.

00:46:05.300 --> 00:46:07.020
Yeah. Yeah.

00:46:07.020 --> 00:46:08.340
Cause humans are human.

00:46:08.340 --> 00:46:09.180
We're fallible.

00:46:09.180 --> 00:46:10.540
We're gonna forget stuff.

00:46:10.540 --> 00:46:12.180
Exactly. Exactly.

00:46:12.180 --> 00:46:13.540
And the story I used to tell a story

00:46:13.540 --> 00:46:15.300
I told 20 years ago at a conference

00:46:15.300 --> 00:46:17.060
talking about root causes,

00:46:17.060 --> 00:46:19.970
I used to lock my keys in the car all the time.

00:46:19.970 --> 00:46:22.210
And I got really good at opening my car

00:46:22.210 --> 00:46:24.050
with a coat hanger.

00:46:24.050 --> 00:46:25.650
But then I got a car after that,

00:46:25.650 --> 00:46:26.690
and that car,

00:46:26.690 --> 00:46:28.610
it was impossible to lock the keys in the car.

00:46:28.610 --> 00:46:30.330
The car wouldn't lock if the keys were inside.

00:46:30.330 --> 00:46:31.610
So the system, I mean,

00:46:32.010 --> 00:46:33.370
people are gonna make errors.

00:46:33.370 --> 00:46:36.560
Somebody said, okay, spy's a dummy.

00:46:36.560 --> 00:46:37.400
We're going to make it

00:46:37.400 --> 00:46:39.360
so he cannot lock his keys in the car.

00:46:39.360 --> 00:46:41.280
He can make different mistakes instead.

00:46:41.280 --> 00:46:42.360
Dude, I'm so sorry to tell you

00:46:42.360 --> 00:46:43.800
this wasn't just about you.

00:46:49.310 --> 00:46:51.390
Ask me about the time I locked the keys

00:46:51.390 --> 00:46:53.390
in a rental car while the car was running

00:46:53.390 --> 00:46:55.420
and I had to call AAA.

00:46:55.420 --> 00:46:56.260
I don't need to ask you,

00:46:56.260 --> 00:46:57.380
you just told the story.

00:47:00.780 --> 00:47:01.820
That was mortifying.

00:47:01.820 --> 00:47:02.740
Oh yeah.

00:47:02.740 --> 00:47:03.900
And I was double parked

00:47:03.900 --> 00:47:06.930
cause I was only stopping for a moment.

00:47:06.930 --> 00:47:08.010
Yep. I've done that.

00:47:08.010 --> 00:47:09.090
I did that before, haven't I?

00:47:09.090 --> 00:47:09.930
Yeah.

00:47:09.930 --> 00:47:10.770
So anyway,

00:47:12.920 --> 00:47:13.840
so excited about the book.

00:47:13.840 --> 00:47:14.680
Narrative fiction,

00:47:14.680 --> 00:47:17.000
models around systems thinking to,

00:47:18.120 --> 00:47:20.840
and so what are examples of some levers?

00:47:20.840 --> 00:47:21.760
What are some of the levers

00:47:21.760 --> 00:47:23.080
to talk about in the book?

00:47:23.080 --> 00:47:24.840
I want to talk about the tiniest levers

00:47:24.840 --> 00:47:27.240
cause this is the last edition before,

00:47:27.240 --> 00:47:29.120
or sorry, the last audition,

00:47:29.120 --> 00:47:30.000
not addition,

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:32.600
audition before the manuscript

00:47:32.600 --> 00:47:34.760
left our hands to go to copy edit,

00:47:34.760 --> 00:47:36.640
which there's this whole funnel

00:47:37.240 --> 00:47:40.240
of activity that goes from idea

00:47:40.240 --> 00:47:43.880
to there are dead trees with your words printed on them

00:47:43.880 --> 00:47:45.040
that you can,

00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:46.520
like there's this whole funnel.

00:47:46.520 --> 00:47:48.320
And one of the critical points

00:47:48.320 --> 00:47:51.120
in that funnel to dead trees

00:47:51.120 --> 00:47:53.000
is the handoff to copy edit.

00:47:53.000 --> 00:47:56.520
And I snuck in one last change under the wire

00:47:56.520 --> 00:47:58.360
and it was about a lever.

00:47:58.360 --> 00:48:00.000
In talking with somebody,

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:03.080
I realized that they were telling a story

00:48:03.080 --> 00:48:04.840
about declining a meeting.

00:48:04.840 --> 00:48:07.200
A lever is any decision that you make.

00:48:07.200 --> 00:48:09.880
And sometimes the tiniest decisions

00:48:09.880 --> 00:48:12.520
actually have a huge outsized impact.

00:48:12.520 --> 00:48:15.960
And so just the act of declining a meeting

00:48:15.960 --> 00:48:18.560
that is going to be a waste of your time

00:48:18.560 --> 00:48:20.960
is in fact pulling a lever on the system.

00:48:20.960 --> 00:48:21.800
You're not,

00:48:21.800 --> 00:48:24.080
you're refusing to feed

00:48:24.080 --> 00:48:27.600
the time wasting way premature

00:48:27.600 --> 00:48:30.000
or 9 million people in their friends

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:31.880
are all invited to the same meeting.

00:48:31.880 --> 00:48:33.880
I call it being CC'd on a meeting.

00:48:34.560 --> 00:48:35.680
Exactly.

00:48:35.680 --> 00:48:38.200
So the big levers that we think of

00:48:38.200 --> 00:48:42.000
are things like the policy lever,

00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:44.840
like you can't merge your code

00:48:44.840 --> 00:48:47.560
until it's passed the pipeline and is green

00:48:47.560 --> 00:48:49.480
or we never ship on red or whatever.

00:48:49.480 --> 00:48:50.640
Those are big levers.

00:48:50.640 --> 00:48:53.160
And they're levers that you need

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:54.360
organizational authority,

00:48:54.360 --> 00:48:57.440
formal authority to enact those levers.

00:48:57.440 --> 00:49:00.280
There are levers that are purely cultural.

00:49:00.280 --> 00:49:02.360
Like one of my wins at Pivotal,

00:49:02.360 --> 00:49:06.080
we were having trouble with long cycle times

00:49:06.080 --> 00:49:08.960
in the test pipeline itself.

00:49:08.960 --> 00:49:11.720
Like the tests took forever.

00:49:11.720 --> 00:49:13.400
They were often flaky.

00:49:13.400 --> 00:49:17.240
And I remember talking with somebody,

00:49:17.240 --> 00:49:19.600
an engineer, I was in VP role.

00:49:19.600 --> 00:49:20.840
I'm talking with a team.

00:49:20.840 --> 00:49:22.400
One of the engineers said something about,

00:49:22.400 --> 00:49:25.320
oh, it's just gonna take so long to clean that up

00:49:25.320 --> 00:49:27.160
and is it really worth it?

00:49:27.160 --> 00:49:28.840
And we're talking about this.

00:49:28.840 --> 00:49:30.760
And I realized that we had a culture problem

00:49:30.760 --> 00:49:33.360
where there was just not this cultural belief

00:49:33.360 --> 00:49:36.600
that it was important to have tight,

00:49:36.600 --> 00:49:40.520
reliable feedback cycles within the pipeline.

00:49:40.520 --> 00:49:44.200
And I don't remember how this came up,

00:49:44.200 --> 00:49:46.560
but I basically said, hey, look,

00:49:46.560 --> 00:49:50.280
if you can cut the execution time down,

00:49:50.280 --> 00:49:52.200
if you can lop an hour or two

00:49:52.200 --> 00:49:55.080
off of the execution time in the pipeline,

00:49:55.080 --> 00:49:56.800
I'll make you a pie.

00:49:56.800 --> 00:49:58.320
And I make good pie.

00:49:59.160 --> 00:50:02.000
Oh, I often think what I need to do for a pie myself.

00:50:02.000 --> 00:50:02.840
Anyway, go on.

00:50:02.840 --> 00:50:06.360
Well, I will make you a pie if we're ever in the same city.

00:50:06.360 --> 00:50:07.880
I don't think it's gonna ship very well

00:50:07.880 --> 00:50:09.920
from where I am to Seattle.

00:50:09.920 --> 00:50:13.360
So this person said, I like pie.

00:50:14.360 --> 00:50:15.560
Everyone likes pie.

00:50:15.560 --> 00:50:17.880
And within a couple of weeks,

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:20.240
he'd done this thing that he'd been

00:50:20.240 --> 00:50:21.800
really resisting doing

00:50:21.800 --> 00:50:25.160
and lopped a couple of hours off of the runtime.

00:50:25.160 --> 00:50:27.640
And I made him a pie.

00:50:27.640 --> 00:50:29.240
And I brought it to stand up.

00:50:29.240 --> 00:50:31.800
It was a very public transference of pie.

00:50:31.800 --> 00:50:32.960
It was a whole pie.

00:50:32.960 --> 00:50:35.120
He's holding this pie in the homemade dish

00:50:35.120 --> 00:50:38.760
and with the crust and the drips and everything.

00:50:38.760 --> 00:50:40.920
And other people are looking at him and saying,

00:50:40.920 --> 00:50:45.410
what did you do to get pie?

00:50:45.410 --> 00:50:46.570
And of course, I turned this into one

00:50:46.570 --> 00:50:49.690
of the best slides ever when I gave this example

00:50:49.690 --> 00:50:50.770
in the talk that I give

00:50:50.770 --> 00:50:52.570
on care and feeding of feedback cycles,

00:50:52.570 --> 00:50:56.100
that it's the pipeline.

00:50:56.100 --> 00:50:58.380
Ah, I get it.

00:50:58.380 --> 00:51:00.300
But in all seriousness, it was a real thing.

00:51:00.300 --> 00:51:02.300
It wasn't just for the jokes.

00:51:02.300 --> 00:51:05.180
It became a tradition.

00:51:05.180 --> 00:51:06.820
And this is a culture lever.

00:51:06.820 --> 00:51:09.220
Now, did the culture lever carry more weight

00:51:09.220 --> 00:51:12.420
because the VP hand baked a pie?

00:51:12.420 --> 00:51:13.260
I don't know.

00:51:13.260 --> 00:51:14.460
I will never know.

00:51:14.460 --> 00:51:16.060
No one will ever tell me.

